    <h1>What Does a Raven Look Like? A Complete Description</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven is a large, entirely black bird with a heavy, arched bill; long, pointed throat hackles (shaggy feathers under the chin); a distinctive wedge-shaped tail visible in flight; and long, broad wings with deeply fingered primaries. In good light its black plumage shows strong blue-violet iridescence. It is roughly twice the size of a crow and unmistakable once the differences are known.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Size</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The Common Raven is the largest passerine (perching bird) in the world:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Body length:</strong> 56–69 cm (22–27 inches)</li>
      <li><strong>Wingspan:</strong> 115–150 cm (45–59 inches)</li>
      <li><strong>Weight:</strong> 700–1,600 g (1.5–3.5 lb) — with northern populations (Alaska, Siberia) at the high end; desert populations at the lower end</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For comparison: the American Crow is 40–53 cm and 300–600 g. A large male raven weighs about three times as much as a large crow.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Plumage</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The entire body, wings, and tail are black, but the quality of the black varies with light and feather wear:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adult iridescence:</strong> in good light, wings and back show strong blue-green, violet, and purple gloss. The iridescence is produced by feather microstructure (thin-film interference), not pigment. The colour shifts visibly as the bird moves.</li>
      <li><strong>Juvenile plumage:</strong> first-year birds (through their first autumn) are a duller, brown-tinged black with little iridescence. Adult gloss is gradually acquired through successive moults over 2–3 years.</li>
      <li><strong>Throat hackles:</strong> the loose, elongated feathers of the throat (the hackles) are distinctive — long, pointed, shaggy-looking. When the bird calls or displays, the hackles are raised, dramatically extending the apparent size of the throat. This is very different from crows, which have shorter, more rounded throat feathers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Bill</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The raven's bill is large and heavy — longer and proportionally deeper than a crow's, with a noticeably curved upper mandible (culmen). The bill is black; the inside of the mouth and gape are pinkish. This heavy bill is a key identification feature at close range: a bird with a truly massive, curved black bill is almost certainly a raven.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Tail Shape</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The wedge-shaped tail is the most reliable in-flight identification feature. When a raven soars or glides, the tail forms a clear diamond or wedge — central feathers visibly longer than outer feathers. Crows have a rounded or square-cut tail. This difference is visible at considerable distance in good conditions.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Wings and Flight Silhouette</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens have long, broad wings with deeply emarginated (deeply notched) primary feathers producing visible "fingers" at the wingtip in soaring flight. The overall silhouette in soaring is a broad-winged, long-tailed cross, distinctly different from the more compact, shorter-winged crow silhouette. Ravens soar extensively, flapping less than crows in level flight.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Key Differences from Crows</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Roughly twice the size</li>
      <li>Wedge-shaped tail (not rounded)</li>
      <li>Much larger, curved bill</li>
      <li>Long, shaggy throat hackles</li>
      <li>Deep "kronk" voice (not "caw")</li>
      <li>More frequent soaring flight</li>
    </ul>
    <p>See <a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs Crow</a> for the full comparison.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
